WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:04.858
For differentiable functions we can find not
just one

2
00:00:04.858 --> 00:00:08.120
derivative but derivatives of the second,
third

3
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.780
and higher orders.

4
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:20.720
The derivative of a function, y′ = f′(x), is
called

5
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:27.570
the first-order derivative. If the function y′
is differentiable,

6
00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:34.230
its derivative is called the second-order
derivative and is denoted by two primes

7
00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:41.140
or d²y/dx². So the second

8
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:44.840
derivative is the derivative of the first
derivative.

9
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.009
The derivative of the second-order derivative,

10
00:00:50.009 --> 00:00:53.540
if it exists, is called the third-order
derivative

11
00:00:53.540 --> 00:01:01.970
and is denoted by three primes or d³y/dx³.

12
00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:05.313
So the third-order derivative is the
derivative of

13
00:01:05.313 --> 00:01:08.590
the second derivative. The derivative of order
n,

14
00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:14.930
or the nth derivative, is the derivative of
the derivative of order n − 1.

15
00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:18.461
Note that, so as not to confuse it with the

16
00:01:18.461 --> 00:01:21.910
nth power, the order of higher derivatives

17
00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:26.250
is written in parentheses. Derivatives of
order higher than the first

18
00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:30.270
are called higher-order derivatives.

19
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:39.180
Let us find the second- and third-order
derivatives of the function y = x⁵.

20
00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:45.720
First, by the rules of differentiation, we
find the first derivative: it equals five

21
00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:52.230
times x to the fourth power. Now we
differentiate the derivative we found: y″

22
00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:56.310
equals 20x³.

23
00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.880
Now let us find the third derivative:

24
00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.340
to do this, we differentiate the second
derivative

25
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.060
and the answer is 60x².

26
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.638
Let us look at another example of finding
higher-order

27
00:02:11.638 --> 00:02:14.370
derivatives: we find all the derivatives

28
00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:20.630
of sin x. First we find the first derivative:
it equals cos x.

29
00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:25.008
In addition, to find the pattern between the
derivative

30
00:02:25.008 --> 00:02:28.590
and its value, we use the reduction formulas.

31
00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:36.180
Now we find the second-order derivative: it
equals −sin x, and

32
00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:43.130
then the third-order derivative: it equals
−cos x. Clearly,

33
00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:48.650
the nth derivative of the sine is expressed by
the following formula.

34
00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.425
Higher-order derivatives can also be found for
parametrically

35
00:02:55.425 --> 00:02:57.800
defined functions. Let the functions

36
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:03.390
be given by parametric equations. As we know,
the first derivative

37
00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:10.130
is found by the formula y′ₓ = y′ₜ /

38
00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:12.410
x′ₜ.

39
00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:15.949
The second derivative is found by the formula:

40
00:03:15.949 --> 00:03:18.950
the derivative of y′ₓ with respect to t

41
00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:25.720
divided by x′ₜ. In the same way, we write the
formula for the third-order derivative

42
00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:30.400
and the formula for the fourth-order
derivative.

43
00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.611
Let us find the second derivative of a
parametrically

44
00:03:37.611 --> 00:03:40.300
defined function. First we find x′ₜ

45
00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:47.260
and y′ₜ and write down the first derivative of
the function.

46
00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:54.080
Now we find the second-order y″ₜ.

47
00:03:54.080 --> 00:04:01.020
We get the value 16t³. Substituting the values
we obtained

48
00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:07.940
into the formula, we get 16t⁴ as the answer.

49
00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.347
So, in this lesson we studied the technique of
finding

50
00:04:14.347 --> 00:04:18.340
higher-order derivatives for various
functions.
